WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:05.280
After a ton of hype about Captain Marvel's
powers, and her potential to take down Thanos,

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:09.460
why did the interstellar heroine have such
a tiny role in Avengers: Endgame?

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:11.800
The film's writers have revealed the real
reason.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:16.400
Avengers: Infinity War teased the arrival
of Carol Danvers in a buzzworthy post-credits

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.800
scene, and Marvel Studios president Kevin
Feige said she would be the most powerful

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.360
character the Marvel Cinematic Universe had
ever seen.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:28.020
Audiences were convinced she would be the
hero to finally bring Thanos to his knees.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.580
But then, she didn't.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:34.380
The film's co-screenwriters Christopher Markus
and Stephen McFeely addressed exactly why

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.380
in a recent deep-dive interview with The New
York Times.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.920
It turns out, Captain Marvel actress Brie
Larson filmed her sequences for Avengers:

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.639
Endgame well before she shot her own solo
movie, and apparently before it even had a

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:47.660
completed script.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.220
Which means that, if you just go by the shooting
schedules, Avengers: Endgame is technically

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:54.480
Carol Danvers' first appearance in the MCU.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:55.880
Imagine that.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:57.260
"Don't you think that's weird?"

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:57.900
"Definitely weird."

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.540
It would be difficult to write an arc for
Captain Marvel in Endgame when her own origin

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.260
story hadn't been pinned down yet.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:05.860
What kind of person is Carol?

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:06.480
What drives her?

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:10.680
And what happens if her motivations between
movies somehow contradict each other?

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.170
Without the foundation of a solo story in
place, the Endgame team apparently decided

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:19.640
to dodge the problem entirely by not including
much of Captain Marvel in their movie.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:23.600
Of course, none of those concerns got in the
way of Black Panther being a pretty major

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.540
player in Captain America: Civil War, but
things were a little bit different with that

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:28.040
film.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.580
While the events of Civil War directly set
up the story of Black Panther, Endgame is

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:37.020
kind of the other way around, picking up with
a modern-day Carol while her story is already

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:37.860
in progress.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.100
McFeely explained,

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:41.940
"We shot [Brie Larson] before she shot her
movie.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:46.520
She's saying lines for a character 20 years
after her origin story, which no one's written

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:47.020
yet.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.140
It's just nuts."

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.920
"We filmed Endgame before Captain Marvel.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.190
We didn't even have a script yet, really,
for Captain Marvel."

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:53.180
"So how does that work?

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:54.020
How do you get into character?"

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:55.980
"It doesn't, but we figured it out."

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:01.660
McFeely also stated that the goal of Avengers:
Endgame wasn't to bring this hotshot new hero

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.540
into the mix and have her save the day.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:07.820
Sure, she came to Tony Stark's rescue at the
top of the movie, and she also swooped in

00:02:07.829 --> 00:02:12.090
to aid the Avengers at a critical moment in
the final battle, using her signature move

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.620
of body-slamming spaceships until they explode.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:19.080
But beyond that Dragon Ball-level spectacle,
Captain Marvel just wasn't a major player

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:20.540
in the defeat of Thanos.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:24.360
Instead, the writers smartly made the choice
to let the Avengers that we've come to know

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:29.060
for years take the spotlight, and in McFeely's
eyes, that's the way it was meant to be.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:29.800
He said,

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:33.420
"Certainly, Captain Marvel is in [Endgame]
a little less than you would have thought.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:37.920
But that's not the story we're trying to tell,
it's the original Avengers dealing with loss

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.220
and coming to a conclusion, and she's the
new, fresh blood."

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:45.880
Markus added that Captain Marvel also had
responsibilities elsewhere in the universe,

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:49.430
so of course she wouldn't be tied up with
the Avengers' big "Time Heist" mission.

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:50.520
Help out where she can?

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:51.020
Definitely.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:52.680
Abandon her other commitments?

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:53.220
No way.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:54.500
As McFeely noted,

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:56.420
"She's been in space nearly half her life.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:57.739
She has obligations."

00:02:58.060 --> 00:02:59.400
"Where the hell have you been all this time?"

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.020
"There are a lot of other planets in the universe,
and unfortunately, they didn't have you guys."

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:09.170
The weird thing is, with the Captain Marvel
movie mostly focusing on Carol becoming a

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:13.820
hero on Earth, audiences really have no idea
what Carol's interstellar obligations look

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:14.320
like.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:17.720
Even weirder is that the writers of Endgame
didn't know much either.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.300
McFeely and Markus' comments are fairly revealing
when it comes to the reality of moviemaking

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:23.000
at Marvel.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:27.579
The studio maps out its film slate years in
advance, and uses each movie as an opportunity

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:28.680
to tease the next.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.880
So far, it's worked exceedingly well.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:37.560
But there's also a slight drawback to this
style of production, in that some films can

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:41.680
end up feeling a little hamstrung by having
to serve the needs of a broader narrative.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:42.620
"What dialect?"

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:43.840
"Wakanada.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:45.740
Wuh...wuh...Wakanda."

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:51.540
MCU filmmakers often have to work their movies
into the larger series in ways that sometimes

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:55.560
come off as a little tacked-on, or at least
hard to weave into a story.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:04:00.280
This appears to be sort of what happened with
Captain Marvel, her solo film, and Endgame.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:04.099
Now that Captain Marvel has come and gone,
fans can almost certainly look forward to

00:04:04.099 --> 00:04:06.220
seeing a lot more of her in future films.

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:09.879
Kevin Feige even teased a second solo movie
when he told Screen Rant,

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:14.300
"The making of a first film is in some ways
a brainstorm on the future and on what can

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:14.800
be.

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:17.020
So what it will be again is not clear.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.220
But what it could be is pretty amazing."

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.650
It's also been confirmed that the first few
years of Marvel's post-Endgame plan will be

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:28.640
officially rolled out in summer 2019, meaning
it won't be long before we find out what the

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:32.900
future is for Captain Marvel, and how big
a part she could play in the next phase of

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:33.600
the MCU.

